  "configuration_id": "config_005",
  "reason": "Evidence: All 6 completed experiments are singleprofile. The best result is config_017 (scvi_n200 + imagenet_vit_l, weighted fusion, acc 0.537), with RNA as the dominant signal (rna_only 0.520 vs image_only ~0.22). The untested multiprofile dimension is the largest source of remaining uncertainty. Question: Does aggregating multiple profiles (via avgpool) improve on the best singleprofile result while keeping the same encoders and fusion? Why this experiment: config_005 is the minimal, direct extension of config_017 into the multiprofile setting—same RNA encoder (scvi_n200), same image encoder (imagenet_vit_l), same fusion (weighted)—with only the addition of multiprofile + avgpool aggregation. This isolates the multiprofile effect cleanly. If it beats 0.537, multiprofile is a productive direction and we can then explore transformer aggregation; if it does not, we avoid wasting budget on more complex multiprofile variants. It is the highest-information single experiment under the limited budget."
}

